Title: Nick and Me
Credit: Written by
Author: Author
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/30/2026
Notes: Contemporary live-action; Milwaukee 1990s into Seattle years later. Closed cast. Peter speaks on-camera in a Seattle frame and in dramatized past; book voice kept tight.

FADE IN:

INT. SEATTLE APARTMENT - NIGHT

Rain streaks a window. City lights smear gray. PETER OLSON, mid-thirties, solid build, short brown hair going early gray at the temples, sits on a couch with an orange-and-white cat. Simple sweater, tired eyes. A half-read notebook on the table.

He looks to camera — calm, plainspoken, Midwest under the polish.

PETER
Big brother was mine. Not the book. My real big brother. Nick.

CUT TO:

EXT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT BUILDING - DAY

A fifth-floor brick walk-up in Milwaukee. Late afternoon. Kids' voices in the alley.

PETER (V.O.)
We lived downtown on Kirk Street after Dad took off. Ma, Nick, and me.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - LIVING ROOM - DAY

MA (OLIVIA), late fifties going frail, brown hair, tired eyes, sits in an overstuffed chair under an afghan, game show on the TV. PETER, early twenties, lean, pizza-job clothes, sets grocery bags down.

NICK fills the doorway — tall, rough-cut, reddish-brown hair that looks slept-on, scars on chin and over left eyebrow, steel in the walk. Burnt-orange energy even standing still.

NICK
What's Ma doin' back in the bedroom at this time of day?

PETER
I don't know, why?

NICK
You got to keep an eye on her. That's how old people go downhill.

Peter nods. Nick already owns the room.

PETER (V.O.)
He had a pair on him the size of steel wrecking balls. People moved out of the way.

EXT. ALLEY BEHIND KIRK STREET - DAY (FLASH, BRIEF)

Kids play kickball off dented garbage-can lids. A ball smashes through a kitchen window. Glass rains.

Young NICK (teen) steps up to furious MRS. ENGEL at the broken pane while little MARK freezes.

NICK
I did it.

PETER (V.O.)
He never told Ma it was Mark. None of us did. Somehow a twelve-year-old came up with sixty bucks for the glass.

INT. MOXIE'S GYM - DAY

Clanging iron. A GUY mid-curl drops a forty-pound bar the second Nick walks in. Offers the bench. Nick nods, loads fifty for warm-ups. Peter watches, smaller, proud and boxed-in.

PETER (V.O.)
Modern-day Viking. Milwaukee blood. He went where he wanted. Nobody questioned that guy.

EXT. PIZZA PLACE SIDEWALK - DAY

Peter locks a bike. SIONNA — short brown hair, pale English-story skin, light blue eyes like pre-dawn sky, black V-neck, fish-symbol necklace, jeans, backpack — trips on cement and brushes his shoulder.

PETER
Are you okay?

SIONNA
Yes.

She brushes her shirt. They look at each other too long.

PETER
I work here. What is your name?

SIONNA
Sionna.

PETER
Sionna? That's different. What does that mean?

SIONNA
Irish goddess. River Shannon.

He isn't really listening. The eyes have him.

PETER
You want to come in? Pizza?

SIONNA
No thanks. I don't eat pizza. But if you want a cup of coffee sometime, you can call me.

She gives a number. Walks away. Looks back over her shoulder once — smooth as a wave.

PETER (V.O.)
It was like I knew her. Intense. Like tides coming in and I didn't want to move my feet.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - KITCHEN - DAY

Ma lines pill bottles, glassy-eyed, mumbling.

MA
Did we go to church today? Did you see Mrs. Canasta?

PETER
Ma, we don't go to church anymore. Your pills are right in front of you.

She snaps back — empty, then sorry. Peter swallows fear.

INT. JOE'S BAR - NIGHT

Packers on the TV. Nick slaps Peter's back.

NICK
Hey, little brother. What's shakin'?

PETER
Ma was all confused. Church. Mrs. Canasta. Empty eyes.

NICK
I knew she'd go crazy cooped up. Take her to the store. You want a nursing home? They take every last dime.

Nick's look could punch. Peter pays and leaves.

EXT. LIBRARY - NIGHT

Peter ducks in out of the cold. Old wood, chain lamps, classic stacks. He fills a temporary card. Pulls WUTHERING HEIGHTS.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - PETER'S ROOM - NIGHT

Peter reads under a weak lamp. Heathcliff, Catherine. Dissolves toward sleep.

PETER (V.O.)
Somehow in the dream it was Sionna's face in old clothes. Running the moors.

INT. JAVA HUT - DAY

College backpacks, steam, talk. Sionna at a back table — green hooded top, those eyes. Peter sits, out of place and locked on.

SIONNA
What are you reading right now?

PETER
Wuthering Heights. Guy and girl grew apart but were really in love.

SIONNA
I know. I read it.

She talks ghosts, doubles, moors — wild, infertile country. Love that never changes destroys.

SIONNA (CONT'D)
A love that never changes will destroy you.

Peter hears Nick in that. Something in him wakes.

PETER (V.O.)
She made me feel smarter just by being around her. We made another date. Dinner. Friday.

EXT. CITY STREET - DAY

Peter walks after coffee, mind loud.

PETER (V.O.)
Maybe that was Ma's problem. Dad left and she never moved on. There ain't no way I could ever forgive him. Nick became my dad when I was eight.

INT. PETER'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Peter bolts upright, sweating.

PETER
Hold!

Dream still on him — forest, brown mare, linen sleeves, embroidered tunic, Sionna's light blue eyes, first kiss in a grassy field.

PETER (V.O.)
Old kings and stuff. It felt like my life for real. Did I shove Wuthering Heights into my head — or was it something else?

INT. JAVA HUT - DAY

Later meetings. Books between them.

SIONNA
Some people think dreaming is real and being awake is not. Buddhists consider everything around us a dream. Not fake — just not lasting. Cycle. Let go. Don't grasp.

PETER
Maybe past life.

SIONNA
Maybe.

He smiles like he belongs somewhere for once.

INT. YOGA STUDIO - DAY

Purple mats. Peter in T-shirt and shorts, shirt falling over his face in downward dog until he tucks it. Breathless, dizzy, groin-stretched — then after savasana, weirdly calm.

SIONNA
You were really great at that.

PETER
Are you kidding me?

SIONNA
Your practice is your practice. That's why they call it practice.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - LIVING ROOM - NIGHT

Nick stomps. Ma in the chair, blank.

NICK
Ma missed her doctor's appointment, you ass. Where the hell were you?

PETER
I totally forgot. I was with someone. I met a girl.

NICK
A girl. That's worth all this? Priorities, Bro.

Door slam. Peter stands in the wreckage of the quiet.

PETER (V.O.)
Would I want Sionna to meet Nick? He wouldn't understand her. She might be afraid of him.

INT. LIBRARY - DAY

Peter checks out yoga and Buddhism. Reads attachment, suffering, present moment.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - LIVING ROOM - DAY

Ma on a game show. Chest odd. Dizzy.

MA
It feels like my heart is going to jump out of my chest.

Peter dials.

INT. DR. CARSON'S OFFICE - DAY

DR. CARSON, serious, faces Peter.

DR. CARSON
Blood pressure dangerously high. Risk of stroke. Emergency room. Observation. IV meds.

INT. HOSPITAL ROOM - DAY

Ma monitored. Sionna brings lunch energy into the hall; Peter's heart lifts. He dozes in a chair.

DREAM HIT — CHAOS: blades, blood, a dying man whispers "Save her." Nick runs in —

DREAM NICK
That you are, little brother.

— and drives a knife into Peter's chest.

Peter gasps awake. Ma dozes. Betrayal sits on him like a coat.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - LIVING ROOM - NIGHT

Blue weekly pill box. Sionna helped. Nick arrives beet-red, boots, smirk.

NICK
Man, you shoulda been a nurse!

PETER
Sionna helped me figure it out.

NICK
This chick sure has you wrapped around her finger.

PETER
You haven't even met her. Give her a break!

NICK
Don't you talk to me like that, you little bastard.

PETER
I'm not a bastard!

WHACK. Nick's hand across Peter's head. Ear rings. Door slam.

PETER (V.O.)
In that split second I knew I never wanted Sionna to meet that prick.

INT. JOE'S BAR - POOL ROOM - NIGHT

Months later. Smoke, AC/DC, quarters on felt. Nick spots ex LINDSEY with a dark-haired DATE. Heat rises. Peter steps in front of Nick.

PETER
Leave it, man. You don't even know this guy.

NICK
Keep out of it. You're next.

Nick's fist draws back toward Peter's face — slow-motion terror — JOE and a BOUNCER crash in. Head-butt. Chaos. Peter runs.

EXT. CITY STREETS - NIGHT

Peter sprints until lungs burn — to Sionna's duplex.

INT. SIONNA'S LIVING ROOM - NIGHT

Green suede sofa. Peter spills it. Sionna strokes his face.

SIONNA
No one who really cares about you does that. You're becoming a bravely compassionate being. Don't let him stop your purpose.

PETER
What is my purpose?

SIONNA
To find the true self within you and honor it. Stay tonight. Nick's out of control. Ma's okay for one night.

He sleeps in her lap. Dream: angel Sionna from lake water; giant Nick with a mace; her force pushes evil back.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - LIVING ROOM - DAY

Time compress: Sionna cooks for Ma; pill box ritual; Peter starts community college; computer in the bedroom; Nick cold then circling back.

NICK
Going to school? This chick tell you what to do? Bro's before ho's.

PETER
I get you, man.

He doesn't.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - LIVING ROOM - DAY

The meeting. Pie. Nick sizes Sionna up.

SIONNA
English major. Philosophy minor. College professor, if I can finish.

NICK
Sometimes school doesn't pay off. Harder for a woman.

SIONNA
Bachelor's degrees for women rose from about thirty-five to fifty-five percent. Diversity helps us learn. You can't keep a good woman down.

She takes plates to the kitchen. Nick steam-stunned. Thin snake smile on the way out. Whisper at the door:

NICK
Take care of Ma.

INT. SEATTLE APARTMENT - NIGHT

Back to frame. Peter rubs the cat's ear.

PETER
Murphy's Law. Just when you think everything's going your way—

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - DAY

Empty. Quiet wrong. Blinking answering machine.

OFFICER DUPUIS (V.O. ON MACHINE)
Mr. Olson, Milwaukee County Sheriff's Department. We need to talk regarding your family.

NURSE (V.O. ON MACHINE)
Frodo Hospital, update on your family members.

INT. POLICE STATION - DAY

Stone 1898 building. OFFICER DUPUIS, graying, thin mustache.

OFFICER DUPUIS
Serious accident. Werner and Lowe. Your brother Nicholas is in serious condition. Your mother, Olivia Olson, passed away at the scene. Toxicology pending. We think he may have been drunk.

Peter's hands shake on a water cup.

INT. HOSPITAL ICU - DAY

Through glass: Nick's wild hair, tubes, machines. Peter looks at the nurse.

PETER
No. I'm not up to seeing him.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - LIVING ROOM - DAY

Ma's empty chair. Afghan. Peter breaks — child-crying, hair in fists.

PETER
Die you fucking jackass. Die.

Sionna holds him.

INT. FUNERAL HOME / EXT. CEMETERY - DAY

Modest casket choices. Few mourners. Dirt hits maple lid — muted thunk. Marker later: Olivia Anne Olsen. 2 Timothy 4:7.

INT. HOSPITAL ROOM - DAY

Nick yellow-bruised, chest tube, leg huge under sheet. Weak voice.

NICK
I guess I really fucked up. Three Jack and Cokes before church. Vehicular manslaughter. Mistake.

PETER
Whose fault was it, Nick?

Nick flares — trapped, harmless in the bed. Peter turns and leaves.

PETER (V.O.)
I no longer gave two shits. I lost my mom. Seemed I lost my brother.

INT. SIONNA'S DUPLEX - LIVING ROOM - NIGHT

Years of strain in a look. Then the door. Nick fills it — thinner from recovery, eyes wrong. Sionna alone.

NICK
You're the reason I'm probably going to jail. You messed with his head. School. Crazy ideas. Money. Stay away from my brother.

SIONNA
This has nothing to do with me. Let's talk rationally.

He backs her to the wall. Hand on her throat. Switchblade flicks open. Point at collarbone.

NICK
Break it off tonight. Cash in a week. Leak one word — I have friends. Parents too.

Front door SLAMS open. PETER.

PETER
Holy shit, Nick— what the fuck are you doing?

Struggle. Blade bites. Red. Sionna sinks. Nick elbows Peter, wipes the knife on her shirt, runs.

Peter on the floor — pressure on the wound, 911, blood everywhere, sirens.

INT. SIONNA'S LIVING ROOM - DAY

Months later. Sionna in the rocking chair, blank ocean eyes. Shoulder healed wrong on the inside.

SIONNA
I can't feel me anymore. As long as there's any doubt, I can't go on with you. Mom and Dad's. Therapist. A break.

PETER
Stay. Please.

She walks to the bedroom. His hands slip off the cliff.

INT. SEATTLE APARTMENT - NIGHT

Frame. Peter alone with the cat, notebook.

PETER
Seven years. Tony's Boy's Pizza — chain in the Northwest. I kept the yoga. Kept the books. Dated almost no one. Hatred for my brother like a second job.

He lies down. Sleep takes him.

DREAM - COUNTRY ROAD - DAY

Impossible green hills. An INDIAN SAGE in white walks beside him. A dirty pizza box on the path.

SAGE
Stop judging. Nothing good or bad but thinking makes it so. Illusion. You create your own reality. Start living the truth.

Sage gone. Box gone. Joy.

INT. SEATTLE APARTMENT - NIGHT

Peter writes the dream fast. Stands.

PETER
I have to see Nick.

EXT. MILWAUKEE STREETS - DAY

Rental car. Old brick fortress on Kirk. Alley dumpsters. Pizza place now CENTRAL COSMETOLOGY. Bob in a deli — thicker, grayer — bear-hugs him proud. Sionna's duplex: naked porch, weeds in gravel. Peter doesn't knock.

EXT. GREEN BAY CORRECTIONAL - DAY

Brick bulk. Wire. Process.

INT. PRISON VISITING BOOTH - DAY

Plexiglass. Phones. NICK sits — older, scrawny, gray at the temples, bags under sad eyes. Weak smile.

NICK
Hi, bro. Glad you came.

PETER
I'm here to tell you some things. Just listen.

(beat)

You're an asshole. You killed my mother drunk. You held me down so you could have the big life. You tried to kill the love of my life and drove her away.

(beat)

And I forgive you. Because it sets me free. Releases your control forever.

Nick's tears hit the counter.

NICK
You're so right. I am a huge asshole. You're more than I ever was.

GUARD (O.S.)
Time.

PETER
I want you to get better. I still believe you can change. After all is said and done, I still love you for being my brother.

NICK
What are you doing now?

PETER
I own a pizza chain in Seattle. You always said I couldn't. I did it, Nick.

NICK
President Olson. I'm so proud of you. I love you.

Guard takes him. Peter hangs up. Light in the chest.

INT. SEATTLE APARTMENT - NIGHT

Final frame. Same window rain. Peter at the glass, softer. Cat against his leg. He almost smiles to camera.

PETER
This has been Nick and me.

(beat)

That's me. President Peter Olson.

FADE OUT.

THE END
